# Git attributes for the Cadenza repo.
#
# ── Concurrent-append merge policy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `spec/semantics/.gate-baseline` (and its per-backend siblings) is the corpus verdict baseline that
# EVERY corpus-adding agent appends to (`cargo xtask gate --save` writes one `verdict<TAB>description`
# line per case). Under the fleet, many agents add cases concurrently, so a plain 3-way merge conflicts
# constantly on this one file — it was the #1 source of merge-request bounces (a fix agent could be
# rejected 4× purely on baseline contention, never touching the same case).
#
# `merge=union` (a BUILT-IN git driver — no registration needed) resolves such a conflict by keeping
# BOTH sides' lines instead of flagging it. That is exactly right here: the file is line-oriented and
# order-independent — `gate --check` parses it into a description-keyed map (see `check_baseline` in
# xtask), and `gate --save` re-sorts + de-dupes on the next write. So concurrent appends of DIFFERENT
# cases union cleanly; the only residue is a possible duplicate line when two agents touch the SAME
# case, which the map-load tolerates and the next `--save` collapses. Net: baseline appends stop
# blocking integration.
spec/semantics/.gate-baseline           merge=union
spec/semantics/.gate-baseline-rust      merge=union
spec/semantics/.gate-baseline-rust-async merge=union
#
# NOTE: the executable-semantics corpus files (`spec/semantics/NN-*.sexp`) are deliberately NOT
# union-merged. Each `(case …)` is a multi-line s-expression BLOCK; unioning interleaved lines from two
# branches would corrupt the s-expr structure (mismatched parens / spliced cases). Those need
# case-level (block) merging or per-vertical file splitting, not line-union — a separate change.
